  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Above normal temperatures and increasing \nchances of showers for the end of the week. \n\nHigh pressure offshore will continue to pump milder and \nincreasingly more humid air across the area through Friday. High\ntemps will be much above normal on Friday. Dry conditions \nshould prevail through midday Friday.\n\nThis will change Friday afternoon as a cold front will pass \nacross the region late Friday and Friday night and will settle \nover Delmarva on Saturday. Scattered showers will develop Friday\nafternoon, mainly north and west of the I-95 corridor, then \nscattered showers spread east Friday night. The showers taper \noff in most areas by Saturday morning, but across southern \nDelaware and south NJ, we'll keep some clouds and scattered \nshowers for Saturday as the front becomes nearly stationary over\nthe Mid- Atlantic. Overall, QPF will be less than 1/10 inch but\na few areas closer to 2/10ths of an inch possible.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled conditions are expected for the later\npart of the weekend. Chances for rain on Sunday have increased.\nCooler and drier into early next week.\n\nHigh pressure then builds into the region from the north on \nSaturday. Mostly sunny skies for most of the area and partly to \nmostly cloudy skies across Delmarva and extreme southern New \nJersey with seasonable, but slightly below normal temperatures \nwith highs in the upper 60s to around 70 degrees.\n\nAfter that, low pressure develops on that stalled front and \npasses cross the Mid-Atlantic and Delmarva Sunday and Sunday \nnight. The overnight models continue the previous trend of \nbringing higher POPs further north for Sunday. The present fcst \nnow has low-end likely POPS for southern Delaware and the 50% \nchance line runs along the Delaware valley and up into Monmouth \nCounty. Low chance pops are included now for the southern \nPoconos and Lehigh Valley\n\nHigh pressure and a much cooler air mass then builds into the \nregion starting Monday into Tuesday. High temperatures Tuesday\nare only near 60 degrees for much of the region, which is\nupwards of 10 degrees below normal. Some patchy frost is\npossible by Tuesday night into Wednesday morning. Forecast lows\nare in the mid 30s north of I-78, and in the low to mid 40s\nelsewhere.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Tidal flooding continues to recede in the back \nbays of New Jersey and Delaware.\n\nTidal levels continue to decline and recede as offshore flow \ncontinues to allow the back bays to drain. Some of the back bays\nof Barnegat Bay, Rehoboth Bay, Jefferson Creek and Little \nAssawoman Bay may see some spotty minor tidal flooding continue \ntoday, as winds turn more shore parallel and slow the rate of \ndrainage. However, the bulk of the threat is over. As a result, \nno coastal flooding headlines are currently in effect.\n\nNo further tidal flooding is expected elsewhere.",
